1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,380
DIMNA: Kalila, if a giant snake stole your 3 eggs every spring, and you were just a tiny crow, how would you stop him?

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:12,100
KALILA: Hmm. I would peck him on the nose! Peck, peck, peck!

3
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:16,740
DIMNA: Peck a giant snake? Ha! No, brother. Today's crow had a much smarter plan.

4
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:28,870
Once upon a time, two crows built a cozy nest, high up in a tall tree. Soon, there were three little eggs in it.

5
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:34,810
MOTHER CROW: Look, my dear! Three eggs! Soon we will have three little babies!

6
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:40,360
But down at the bottom of the tree, in a dark hole, lived a big, greedy snake.

7
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:47,040
SNAKE: Hsss... Eggs! I smell eggs! Yummy eggs for my breakfast!

8
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:53,600
Every spring, the snake crept up the tree and stole the crows' eggs. And now, here he came again!

9
00:00:54,150 --> 00:00:56,520
FATHER CROW: Go away, snake! Leave our eggs alone!

10
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:03,170
SNAKE: Hsss! You are too small to stop me. I will come back tomorrow!

11
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:10,420
MOTHER CROW: Oh no! What can we do? He is so big, and we are so small.

12
00:01:10,870 --> 00:01:15,380
FATHER CROW: Don't cry, my dear. Let's ask our friend, the wise old jackal. He always knows what to do.

13
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:22,590
So the father crow flew to the wise jackal and told him everything.

14
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:28,100
WISE JACKAL: Hmm. Don't fight the snake with your little beak. Use your head instead!

15
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:36,740
WISE JACKAL: Fly to the king's garden. Find something shiny, and make sure the guards see you take it. Then drop it into the snake's hole.

16
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:39,970
FATHER CROW: Into the snake's hole? Oh! I see! What a clever plan!

17
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:47,660
In the king's garden, the princess had left her shiny necklace on a stone, beside the pool.

18
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:52,000
Swoosh! Down flew the crow, and he picked it up with his beak.

19
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:56,000
GUARD: Hey! That crow took the princess's necklace! After him!

20
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:03,200
The crow flew slowly, slowly, so the guards could follow him, all the way back to the tall tree.

21
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:05,860
FATHER CROW: This way! Follow me! Not too fast...

22
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:11,240
Then, plop! He dropped the necklace right into the snake's hole.

23
00:02:11,790 --> 00:02:14,840
GUARD: It fell in that hole! Let's get it out with my stick!

24
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:19,110
SNAKE: Hsss! Who is poking my house?

25
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,430
GUARD: A snake! Shoo! Shoo! Go away, and never come back!

26
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:28,310
SNAKE: Hsss! Help! All right, all right, I'm going! I'm going!

27
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:36,330
And the snake slid away, far, far away, and he never came back. The guards took the necklace home to the princess.

28
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:42,440
And soon, crack, crack, crack! Three baby crows came out of their eggs.

29
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:44,030
BABY CROWS: Caw! Caw! Caw!

30
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:49,980
MOTHER CROW: Thank you, wise jackal! A clever head is better than a big beak!

31
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:58,840
KALILA: So the little crows could not fight the big snake. But they used their heads, and the bully had to go!

32
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:02,220
DIMNA: Cleverness beats a bully! And nobody got hurt at all.

33
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:07,340
DIMNA: Persian word of the day! In Persian, a snake is maar. Maar! Hsss!

34
00:03:07,490 --> 00:03:11,490
KALILA: Maar. A snake. But don't worry, our maar is gone for good.

35
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:18,170
KALILA: And next time: a hungry heron, a clever crab, and a lake full of fish.

